{"subjectId":"sql","id":"date-time-functions","title":"Date & Time Functions","group":"Functions & Aggregation","order":22,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Dates are the least portable part of SQL. The concepts — extract a part, add an interval, difference two dates, truncate to a period — exist everywhere under different names.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-language\"></i>Dialect map</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>SQLite</th><th>PostgreSQL</th><th>MySQL</th><th>SQL Server</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Now</td><td><code>datetime('now')</code></td><td><code>now()</code></td><td><code>NOW()</code></td><td><code>GETDATE()</code> / <code>SYSDATETIME()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Today</td><td><code>date('now')</code></td><td><code>CURRENT_DATE</code></td><td><code>CURDATE()</code></td><td><code>CAST(GETDATE() AS date)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Year of d</td><td><code>strftime('%Y', d)</code></td><td><code>EXTRACT(YEAR FROM d)</code></td><td><code>YEAR(d)</code></td><td><code>YEAR(d)</code> / <code>DATEPART(year, d)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Add 1 month</td><td><code>date(d, '+1 month')</code></td><td><code>d + INTERVAL '1 month'</code></td><td><code>DATE_ADD(d, INTERVAL 1 MONTH)</code></td><td><code>DATEADD(month, 1, d)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Days between</td><td><code>julianday(b) - julianday(a)</code></td><td><code>b - a</code> (dates)</td><td><code>DATEDIFF(b, a)</code></td><td><code>DATEDIFF(day, a, b)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Start of month</td><td><code>date(d, 'start of month')</code></td><td><code>date_trunc('month', d)</code></td><td><code>DATE_FORMAT(d, '%Y-%m-01')</code></td><td><code>DATETRUNC(month, d)</code> (2022)</td></tr><tr><td>Format</td><td><code>strftime('%d/%m/%Y', d)</code></td><td><code>to_char(d, 'DD/MM/YYYY')</code></td><td><code>DATE_FORMAT(d, '%d/%m/%Y')</code></td><td><code>FORMAT(d, 'dd/MM/yyyy')</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">SQLite</div><p>SQLite stores dates as ISO-8601 text (<code>'2024-03-15'</code>, <code>'2024-03-15 09:30:00'</code>), which sorts and compares correctly as strings. Its date functions take such a string plus <em>modifiers</em>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calendar-event\"></i>Extracting parts</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT hire_date,\n       strftime('%Y', hire_date)           AS year,\n       CAST(strftime('%m', hire_date) AS INTEGER) AS month,\n       strftime('%w', hire_date)           AS weekday_0_sun,\n       strftime('%d/%m/%Y', hire_date)     AS indian_format\nFROM employees\nWHERE emp_id IN (1, 2, 3)\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>hire_date  | year | month | weekday_0_sun | indian_format\n-----------+------+-------+---------------+--------------\n2015-03-01 | 2015 | 3     | 0             | 01/03/2015\n2017-06-15 | 2017 | 6     | 4             | 15/06/2017\n2019-01-10 | 2019 | 1     | 4             | 10/01/2019</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calendar-plus\"></i>Date arithmetic</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT date('2024-01-31', '+1 month')             AS plus_month,\n       date('2024-03-15', '-10 days')             AS minus_days,\n       date('2024-03-15', 'start of month')       AS month_start,\n       date('2024-03-15', 'start of month', '+1 month', '-1 day') AS month_end,\n       julianday('2024-03-01') - julianday('2024-02-01')          AS days_in_feb;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>plus_month | minus_days | month_start | month_end  | days_in_feb\n-----------+------------+-------------+------------+------------\n2024-03-02 | 2024-03-05 | 2024-03-01  | 2024-03-31 | 29.0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Month arithmetic has edge cases: SQLite turns <code>2024-01-31 + 1 month</code> into <code>2024-03-02</code> (Feb 31 overflows). PostgreSQL and MySQL clamp to the last day (<code>2024-02-29</code>). Test month-end logic explicitly.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hourglass\"></i>Tenure and age</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>-- as of a fixed reporting date, so the output never changes\nSELECT name, hire_date,\n       CAST((julianday('2024-12-31') - julianday(hire_date)) / 365.25 AS INTEGER) AS years_service\nFROM employees\nORDER BY hire_date\nLIMIT 4;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>name   | hire_date  | years_service\n-------+------------+--------------\nAsha   | 2015-03-01 | 9\nPriya  | 2016-11-20 | 8\nRavi   | 2017-06-15 | 7\nVikram | 2018-09-05 | 6</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-calendar-stats\"></i>Grouping by period</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT strftime('%Y-%m', o.order_date)          AS month,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT o.order_id)                AS orders,\n       SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price)          AS revenue\nFROM orders o\nJOIN order_items oi ON oi.order_id = o.order_id\nWHERE o.status &lt;&gt; 'cancelled'\nGROUP BY month\nORDER BY month;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>month   | orders | revenue\n--------+--------+--------\n2024-01 | 2      | 66700\n2024-02 | 2      | 12900\n2024-03 | 3      | 63700</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Store timestamps in <strong>UTC</strong> (<code>TIMESTAMPTZ</code> in PostgreSQL, <code>DATETIME2</code>/<code>DATETIMEOFFSET</code> in SQL Server) and convert to local time only for display.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Performance</div><p>Wrapping an indexed column in a function (<code>WHERE strftime('%Y', order_date) = '2024'</code>) usually prevents index use. Rewrite as a range: <code>WHERE order_date &gt;= '2024-01-01' AND order_date &lt; '2025-01-01'</code>.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you find orders placed in the last 30 days?</summary><div><p><code>WHERE order_date &gt;= CURRENT_DATE - INTERVAL '30 days'</code> (PostgreSQL), <code>&gt;= DATE_SUB(CURDATE(), INTERVAL 30 DAY)</code> (MySQL), <code>&gt;= DATEADD(day, -30, CAST(GETDATE() AS date))</code> (SQL Server), <code>&gt;= date('now', '-30 days')</code> (SQLite). Keep the column bare so an index on it can be used.</p></div></details></section>"}